A recursive call can ask for the answer starting at each possible next index. Without saving results, the same indexes are solved many times. The `memo` array avoids that repeat work.

The base case is simple: if the current index is already at or beyond the last position, no more jumps are needed. The problem guarantees that the end can be reached, so every explored branch has a valid result.

```cpp
class Solution {
private:
    int findMinimum(int index, vector<int>& nums, vector<int>& memo) {
        int n = nums.size();
        if (index >= n - 1) {
            return 0;
        }

        if (memo[index] != -1) {
            return memo[index];
        }

        int best = n;
        int lastReach = min(n - 1, index + nums[index]);

        for (int next = index + 1; next <= lastReach; next++) {
            best = min(best, 1 + findMinimum(next, nums, memo));
        }

        memo[index] = best;
        return best;
    }

public:
    int jump(vector<int>& nums) {
        if (nums.size() <= 1) {
            return 0;
        }

        vector<int> memo(nums.size(), -1);
        return findMinimum(0, nums, memo);
    }
};
```

A useful edge case is an array of length one, where the answer is immediately zero. The recursion depth can be linear, so this uses `O(n)` stack space in addition to the memo table.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` in the worst case.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the memo table and recursion stack.

Instead of starting at index zero, this code starts from the destination. At each earlier position, it checks whether a jump can land on each later position and uses the already-filled answer for that position.

The last index needs zero jumps. The answer for index zero is ready after all positions to its right have been processed.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int jump(vector<int>& nums) {
        int n = nums.size();
        if (n <= 1) {
            return 0;
        }

        vector<int> jumpsFrom(n, n);
        jumpsFrom[n - 1] = 0;

        for (int index = n - 2; index >= 0; index--) {
            int farthest = min(n - 1, index + nums[index]);

            for (int landing = index + 1; landing <= farthest; landing++) {
                if (jumpsFrom[landing] != n) {
                    jumpsFrom[index] = min(
                        jumpsFrom[index],
                        jumpsFrom[landing] + 1
                    );
                }
            }
        }

        return jumpsFrom[0];
    }
};
```

For `[2, 3, 1, 1, 4]`, the last position starts at `0`. Index `3` can reach it in one jump, then index `1` can use index `3` or jump directly to the end. The single-element case returns zero before creating the table.

The array is only read, not modified.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` in the worst case.
Space complexity: `O(n)`.

This version fills a table from right to left. `minimumJumps[i]` stores the fewest jumps needed to get from index `i` to the end. For each index, all positions that one jump can reach are checked, and the smallest result is kept.

The table makes the choices easy to follow, but it uses more memory and can check many pairs of indices.

```java
class Solution {
    int jump(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        if (n <= 1) {
            return 0;
        }

        int[] minimumJumps = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
            minimumJumps[i] = n;
        }
        minimumJumps[n - 1] = 0;

        for (int index = n - 2; index >= 0; index--) {
            int lastReach = Math.min(n - 1, index + nums[index]);

            for (int next = index + 1; next <= lastReach; next++) {
                minimumJumps[index] = Math.min(
                    minimumJumps[index],
                    1 + minimumJumps[next]
                );
            }
        }

        return minimumJumps[0];
    }
}
```

For example, the final position starts with value `0`. Every earlier position adds one jump to a value already computed. The input array is not changed.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` in the worst case.
Space complexity: `O(n)`.

Here the indexes are treated like nodes in a shortest-path search. A queue stores positions reached after the same number of jumps. When an index is removed, all of its next reachable indexes are added once. The first time the last index is found, its jump count is the minimum.

A `visited` array is important. Without it, several earlier positions could add the same index many times.

```javascript
function jump(nums) {
  const lastIndex = nums.length - 1;
  if (lastIndex <= 0) {
    return 0;
  }

  const queue = [0];
  const visited = new Array(nums.length).fill(false);
  visited[0] = true;
  let head = 0;
  let jumps = 0;

  while (head < queue.length) {
    const levelSize = queue.length - head;
    jumps++;

    for (let count = 0; count < levelSize; count++) {
      const index = queue[head++];
      const lastReach = Math.min(lastIndex, index + nums[index]);

      for (let next = index + 1; next <= lastReach; next++) {
        if (next === lastIndex) {
          return jumps;
        }

        if (!visited[next]) {
          visited[next] = true;
          queue.push(next);
        }
      }
    }
  }

  return 0;
}
```

For `[2, 3, 1, 1, 4]`, the first level reaches indexes `1` and `2`. Processing that level reaches index `4`, so the function returns `2`.

This is easier to connect to regular BFS, but it needs `O(n)` extra space. The guaranteed reachability means the final fallback is not normally used.

Time complexity: `O(n)` because each index is added and processed at most once.
Space complexity: `O(n)`.

